    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Benelli Showed the World Its New Tornado Naked Tre 903 Concept at EICMA 2026 and It's a Triple!\nMercedes Recalls A-Class Through GLC Line Over Faulty Door Lock Microswitches\n69 photos\nPhoto: Mercedes-Benz\nMercedes-Benz AG has a bit of a problem with a component many drivers never even think about until their cars refuse to engage the electronic parking brake. A microswitch inside the driver's door lock can stop recognizing when someone opens the door if enough moisture builds up in there, compromising the auto-park function as well.\nPer the State Administration for Market Regulation, the Stuttgart-based automaker is recalling a whopping 180,277 vehicles over this defect in the People's Republic of China. This recall actually consists of two separate campaigns. Beijing Mercedes-Benz Automotive is listed with 129,819 locally assembled models, while the second campaign targets 50,458 imported vehicles.\nBeijing Mercedes-Benz Automotive lists production dates between February 8, 2018, and June 1, 2020, for the locally produced A-Class, GLA, and GLB . The other population comprises the A-, B-, and C-Class, CLA, CLE, GLA, GLC, and GLC lines, with production dates ranging from April 21, 2018, through February 25, 2026. In both cases, the affected driver's door lock assemblies will be replaced from February 2027 onward.\nThe microswitch's potting material is the weak point, allowing water to get in there. Insufficient resistance to moisture results in a corroded electrical switch. In this state, the vehicle may no longer correctly recognize if the driver has opened or closed the door. Because the electronic parking brake and auto-park function rely on that information, both functions could malfunction.\nThis is not Merc's first microswitch rodeo, though. Back in July 2026, the company recalled no fewer than 310,667 such vehicles in the United States of America under NHTSA campaign 26V481. Affected models included the A-Class, CLA, C-Class, GLA, GLB, and the GLC to boot. Instead of repairing the microswitch, dealers were instructed to fully replace the door lock assembly.\nPhoto: Mercedes-Benz Australia followed suit with campaign REC-006695 in August 2026, covering just under 59,000 vehicles from the 2018 through 2026 model years. The local regulator described the very same vulnerability and safety risks.\nIn addition to said microswitch recall, the German automaker has also identified a problem that affects only two electric CLAs produced in China for the Chinese market. Assembled by Beijing Mercedes-Benz on November 25, 2026, and December 3, 2026, said models are suspected of damaged electrical components on the circuit board inside the rear drive module's power electronics unit.\nA potential electrical short may interrupt power transmission, and you clearly don't want such a loss of motive power when driving at high speeds. These vehicles will have their affected modules replaced at no charge to affected customers. Also worth noting, both CLAs qualify for immediate attention.\nWaiting until February 2027 for the scheduled campaign to begin in China is understandable, but only just. Considering that 180,277 vehicles have to be inspected, Merc's local subsidiary must stock up on replacement door lock assemblies efficiently in only a few short months.\nThe logistical effort is massive, and replacing so many door locks definitely takes a lot of time. In the meantime, potentially affected customers may get in touch with Beijing Mercedes-Benz Automobile and Mercedes-Benz Automobile Sales by phone for additional information regarding parts availability.\nVia // SAMR\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMercedes-Benz recall China EV safety\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT One-Owner 1963 Plymouth Fury Ragtop Sat 20 Years in a Barn, 140k-Mile Poly V8 Can Still Do a Burnout",
